[heading Overview]

Boost.Taar is a portable, header-only C++20 library that provides
ergonomic building blocks for writing HTTP servers on top of
__Asio__, __Beast__, __Url__, and __Json__. It is licensed under the
Boost Software License, Version 1.0.

The library is organised around four ideas:

* a [link boost_taar.intro.matchers /matcher DSL/] for selecting which
  handler should serve a given HTTP request,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.handlers /handler/] family that turns ordinary
  callables into HTTP responders, with automatic argument extraction
  from path templates, query parameters, headers, cookies, and request
  bodies,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.session session coroutine] that owns the
  request/response lifecycle for one keep-alive connection,
* a [link boost_taar.intro.server TCP server coroutine] that accepts
  connections and dispatches them to the session.

On top of the HTTP layer sit three optional protocol layers that reuse
the same callables and state: a JSON-RPC 2.0 endpoint, a Model Context
Protocol (MCP) server endpoint, and an OpenAPI 3.1 document generator.
Handlers may also receive per-connection or per-session state objects by
simply declaring a parameter of the right type.

All of it is asynchronous by construction. The session and the TCP
acceptor are C++20 coroutines, and a request handler can itself be a
coroutine: declare its result as `taar::awaitable<T>` and `co_await`
a database query, an outbound HTTP call, or a timer before responding,
without ever blocking the connection. Long or open-ended responses can
stream incrementally through an async generator. See
[link boost_taar.design.async the asynchronous model] for the full
picture.

[heading At a glance]

Each block below is a complete registration; the last one runs them.
They share these declarations:

    namespace taar = boost::taar;
    namespace http = boost::beast::http;
    using taar::matcher::method;
    using taar::matcher::target;

    taar::session::http http_session;

[*REST.] Path placeholders are parsed into the callable's parameter
types and the result becomes the response; here a JSON object
([link boost_taar.quickstart.tour Tour]):

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}",
        taar::handler::rest(
            [](int a, int b) { return boost::json::value {{"result", a + b}}; },
            taar::handler::path_arg("a"),
            taar::handler::path_arg("b")));

[*JSON-RPC 2.0.] An endpoint owns its methods; batches, notifications and
error codes are handled for you:

    taar::jsonrpc::endpoint<> rpc;
    rpc.method("sum", [](int a, int b) { return a + b; },
        taar::jsonrpc::rpc_arg("a"), taar::jsonrpc::rpc_arg("b"));

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::post && target == "/rpc", rpc.handler());

[*MCP.] Tools get an input schema derived from their parameters, and a
stateful endpoint hands each client session its own state:

    struct notes { std::vector<std::string> items; };
    taar::mcp::endpoint<notes> mcp {{.name = "notes", .version = "1.0.0"}};
    mcp.tool("notes/add", "Append a note",
        [](notes& n, std::string text) { n.items.push_back(text); return n.items.size(); },
        taar::mcp::mcp_arg("text", "The note"));

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/mcp", mcp.stream_handler());
    http_session.register_request_handler(target == "/mcp", mcp.handler());

[*Static files.] A directory served with validators, ranges,
precompressed variants and a single-page-app fallback:

    taar::handler::htdocs_options site;
    site.root = "./public";
    site.fallback_document = "index.html";
    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/{*path}",
        taar::handler::htdocs {std::move(site)});

[*Running it.] One coroutine accepts connections for every route above;
`signals.emit()` shuts it down gracefully:

    boost::asio::io_context io;
    taar::cancellation_signals signals;
    boost::asio::co_spawn(
        io,
        taar::server::tcp("0.0.0.0", "8080", http_session, signals),
        boost::asio::detached);
    io.run();

The same callables can also be documented with the OpenAPI generator,
served over TLS, or streamed as chunked responses. See the
[link boost_taar.quickstart Quick Start] for a walk-through, the
[link boost_taar.examples Examples] for complete programs, and the
[link boost_taar.quickref Quick Reference] for every public name.

